WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Welcome to the test set.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:08.600
Here we talk with some of the brightest thinkers and tinkerers in statistical analysis, scientific

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:14.080
computing, and machine learning, digging into what makes them tick, plus the insights, experiments,

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:19.120
and OMG moments that shape the field.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:22.360
When I asked Joe Cheng what gets him out of bed in the morning, he told me it alternates

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.719
between duty, curiosity, and fear.

00:00:25.719 --> 00:00:30.000
Joe is the CTO of Posit and the creator of Shiny, a dashboarding tool heavily used in

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.599
the R&P communities.

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:37.119
In this episode, Joe unpacks that answer — the quiet routine of duty days, what it means

00:00:37.119 --> 00:00:42.160
to be a reluctant leader, and why building AI tools today feels like excitement mixed

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.080
with existential dread.

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.240
We're joined by his longtime collaborator and my co-host Hadley Wickham.

00:00:48.240 --> 00:00:52.919
They're opposites in a lot of ways — Hadley all conviction, Joe all caution — but they've

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:55.240
spent a decade riffing off one another.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:01:00.919
And honestly, I love their dynamic, so I'm so excited for you to listen to Joe Cheng.

00:01:00.919 --> 00:01:06.040
All right, Joe, welcome to the test set.

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:12.360
I feel like this is a really stacked interview because obviously you're the CTO at Posit,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.440
joined by our fabulous co-host Hadley, who's chief scientist at Posit.

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:23.320
I'm really excited about, honestly, having worked a bit with the Shiny team, your work

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.839
on Shiny, but also so much more stuff.

00:01:28.839 --> 00:01:34.279
You work as CTO at Posit and obviously everything you're doing with AI.

00:01:34.279 --> 00:01:36.360
So just super excited to have you on.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:38.800
Yeah, glad to be here.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:43.320
Maybe even just kick it off, could you tell us a bit about how you and Hadley have interacted

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:44.320
over the years?

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:46.760
Do you hang out?

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:47.760
What's that like?

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:48.760
Yeah.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:54.320
Yeah, I think Hadley and I, we started working together in 2012, when you joined us at our

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.760
studio at the time.

00:01:56.760 --> 00:02:04.480
And it's a little embarrassing how funny this is going to be, but one of our first

00:02:04.480 --> 00:02:11.839
interactions — and me not really knowing what to expect from you, just being the creator

00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:15.839
of ggplot, and that was about all I knew, and someone who could help us kind of bring

00:02:15.839 --> 00:02:22.360
some taste, like our data scientist taste, to what we were doing.

00:02:22.360 --> 00:02:25.880
But you asked me a question about parsing CSVs.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:29.160
Do you remember this?

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.080
And I gave you basically the beginning of an explanation.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:40.279
And I said, you want to use a deterministic finite automata, and you can think of it as

00:02:40.279 --> 00:02:45.199
moving through a few states, and you were basically like, got it.

00:02:45.199 --> 00:02:47.839
And I was like, okay, come back when you have questions.

00:02:47.839 --> 00:02:52.399
And you came back with like, you mean like this, and you had fully sort of mapped it

00:02:52.399 --> 00:02:53.399
out.

00:02:53.399 --> 00:03:01.000
And I think that pattern has been pretty consistent with us working together, is that it feels

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:07.279
like when I work with Hadley, I just have to say the beginning part of a sentence, and

00:03:07.279 --> 00:03:08.279
he's like, I got it.

00:03:08.279 --> 00:03:10.839
And he actually gets it.

00:03:10.839 --> 00:03:11.839
Whereas I think most people —

00:03:11.839 --> 00:03:13.600
Like, stop talking, I'm bored.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.520
Yeah, yeah, say less, say less, yeah.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:22.320
Whereas I think especially at the time, a lot of people that I was working with that

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:28.080
did not come from a software engineering background, they came from more data.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.520
I was just used to, okay, I'm going to have to repeat this a number of times, we're going

00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:38.399
to have to approach it at multiple levels of abstraction before they kind of fully understand.

00:03:38.399 --> 00:03:45.779
And with Hadley, it's always been this shorthand.

00:03:45.779 --> 00:03:52.679
And that really carried into when Hadley wrote Mastering Shiny, the book that I wanted to

00:03:52.679 --> 00:03:55.639
write but was not able to.

00:03:55.639 --> 00:04:00.520
And Hadley, you know, it's easy for him to write books, it's easy for you to write books,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:09.000
but you were missing so much, not just details about Shiny, but the details about why things

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:11.320
were a certain way.

00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:18.320
And I don't think I could have transferred the information to someone else, because there

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.399
was so much information to transfer.

00:04:20.399 --> 00:04:28.600
But I felt like with you, Hadley, it was like, in 15 minutes, I could convey months of work

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:36.399
of, you know, really intricate details about the way reactivity works, and why there's

00:04:36.399 --> 00:04:40.160
sort of like this push pull effects to reactivity.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:44.399
And I really felt like for someone else, each one of those points would have taken like

00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.160
a couple of days to really get them to understand.

00:04:47.160 --> 00:04:51.920
And with Hadley, it was always just like, okay, I got it.

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:59.799
Push is, it's a very, it's an exciting way to work, I think, when like we're able to

00:04:59.799 --> 00:05:04.679
transfer that kind of context, like almost as fast as we can, we can talk.

00:05:04.679 --> 00:05:10.880
And I hope that like, when we work together, I'm able to hold my own enough that, you know,

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.880
it's a pleasant experience for Hadley also.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:19.200
No, I think one of my like, biggest regrets of like, Studio Stage Positive is just that

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:25.079
we haven't had like more projects to work on together that we could like really crank on.

00:05:25.079 --> 00:05:28.880
Because that's, yeah, like I love writing Mastering Shiny too, because I'm like, okay,

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:32.399
yeah, I can ask these questions, and I get clear, obvious answers back.

00:05:32.399 --> 00:05:34.399
And that makes sense.

00:05:34.399 --> 00:05:39.480
And I can like push you on bits where I'm like, I like, I wouldn't have done it this way.

00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:45.160
I mean, get like, get answers that I'm like, okay, yeah, that's, I didn't think about that.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:50.839
From the outside, like having seen Mastering Shiny before I knew the context, it was so

00:05:50.839 --> 00:05:54.720
surprising, like, just to be like, Hadley, what are you doing here?

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.399
Like Mastering Shiny, but it is so valuable.

00:06:00.399 --> 00:06:05.480
Yeah, I could see the value of like really picking up something you're like less familiar

00:06:05.480 --> 00:06:10.959
with and that that transfers to people who are reading it, that you had to kind of really

00:06:10.959 --> 00:06:17.320
wrap your mind around it and versus a person who built the thing and kind of knows it intimately,

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:18.320
figure it out.

00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:22.359
Joe is one of the many people I've given my advice to, like writing a book is easy.

00:06:22.359 --> 00:06:24.679
You just have to write for like an hour a day.

00:06:24.679 --> 00:06:29.119
And then like after a year, like you'll have a book, but I don't believe like anyone has

00:06:29.119 --> 00:06:31.959
successfully followed that advice to complete the book.

00:06:31.959 --> 00:06:35.440
So it's clearly not good advice.

00:06:35.440 --> 00:06:40.640
Hadley, do you think about like, when you think about throwing things at Joe, like what,

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:44.320
what types of problems stand out to you as like Joe shaped problems?

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:45.320
What would you be like?

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:47.839
We got to put Joe on this.

00:06:47.839 --> 00:06:55.920
Thinking back to the early days, like coming from like academia to Posit, it was just amazing

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:59.279
to feel like my ideas were like respected.

00:06:59.279 --> 00:07:06.600
I was like, Oh, like, like these people just like believe what I am doing is like useful

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:12.720
and important, just so like freeing and then be able to be able to like interact with them

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.320
on that like technical level.

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:22.000
And to never, there's definitely this like kind of, I sometimes feel myself falling back

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.920
into this when I like visit universities, but there's kind of like academic one-upsmanship

00:07:26.920 --> 00:07:31.239
where you're like, you know, everyone's a little insecure in their positions.

00:07:31.239 --> 00:07:36.399
So it's about like making sure you're kind of like, you know, making yourself feel good

00:07:36.399 --> 00:07:39.679
and other people feel not so good.

00:07:39.679 --> 00:07:43.640
Because like one of the things I felt when I was in academia, it was like so hard to

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:49.160
ask questions about things that I did not know, that it felt like every statistician

00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.519
should know.

00:07:50.519 --> 00:07:53.200
And there was like never that at RStudio.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:58.480
Like I could ask Joe and JJ what seemed like the dumbest questions.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:02.320
And they were just like endlessly patient with me.

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.760
And it didn't like, it didn't feel like they were like, Oh, what an idiot.

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:06.760
He doesn't know this.

00:08:06.760 --> 00:08:07.760
They're just like, yeah.

00:08:07.760 --> 00:08:11.720
Like people are fairly good at some things and don't know about other things.

00:08:11.720 --> 00:08:13.200
It's just, yeah.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:14.200
All in stuff together.

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:20.640
That was, that was like, so that was just an incredibly like freeing experience for me.

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:21.640
Yeah.

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:22.640
Yeah.

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:26.880
I could see that coming out of like those like classic academic brown bags where you're

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:30.359
getting like hammered at the end.

00:08:30.359 --> 00:08:31.839
Yeah.

00:08:31.839 --> 00:08:37.039
Um, I, yeah, I'm curious.

00:08:37.039 --> 00:08:42.280
Joe I know you probably gave me one of the most intriguing answers to a question I asked

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.880
before this, which was what gets you out of bed in the morning?

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:53.760
And you said a mix of curiosity, duty and or fear.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:58.000
I, I was going to ask you and have you repeat that, but I'm actually so intrigued.

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:03.000
I thought I'd just lay it out there and, and try to get your take because that I've been

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.000
just haunted by this statement.

00:09:06.000 --> 00:09:08.039
And I'm so curious about that.

00:09:08.039 --> 00:09:09.039
Yeah.

00:09:09.039 --> 00:09:14.039
So, um, I think on my best days, it's curiosity on the best days.

00:09:14.039 --> 00:09:18.960
It's like, I have this burning idea and I can't wait to see if it works.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:21.320
Those days are not often.

00:09:21.320 --> 00:09:28.200
But when they happen, not only are they so fun and so exciting, but often those are the

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:35.940
days where there's like a big breakthrough, you know, um, and once in a while, if lightning

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:39.919
really strikes, you know, the early days of shiny, it was like that every day.

00:09:39.919 --> 00:09:45.359
Uh, the, the early days of working on, uh, LLM APIs was like that every day.

00:09:45.359 --> 00:09:47.799
Um, and that's, those are just like highlights.

00:09:48.799 --> 00:09:53.880
I think like 90% of days it's duty that it gets me out of bed.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:58.080
It's just this feeling like the company's counting on me.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:09:59.640
My team's counting on me.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.799
My family's counting on me.

00:10:00.799 --> 00:10:05.640
Our users are counting on me and, uh, it's just time to get up and make the doughnuts,

00:10:05.640 --> 00:10:06.640
you know?

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:13.400
Um, and there is something that I think is very noble about that.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:19.359
There's something that I think there's something very satisfying about just quietly doing your

00:10:19.359 --> 00:10:22.679
job when there's no glamor in it.

00:10:22.679 --> 00:10:29.119
Um, it just, that just feels like that's most days and that's okay.

00:10:29.119 --> 00:10:30.119
You know?

00:10:30.119 --> 00:10:39.119
Um, and then there's the fear days where I'm, I'm driven by a sense of anxiety or dread

00:10:39.119 --> 00:10:46.760
about a got to stay ahead of our competitors, got like the, or the fear of like being embarrassed

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:53.039
on stage, you know, or the fear that we're, that I'm personally falling behind in adopting

00:10:53.039 --> 00:10:56.239
some new technology or understanding some new hot concepts.

00:10:56.239 --> 00:11:03.880
Uh, those are the worst, but fear days, I think they can easily lead to working really

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:06.320
hard in unproductive directions.

00:11:06.320 --> 00:11:10.640
Um, or sometimes you can counterproductive directions because like the impulse to do

00:11:10.640 --> 00:11:18.000
something is so strong as like a way to like cathartic cathartically bat away the fear.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:27.000
Um, and unfortunately I feel like there's been way, a disproportionate amount of fear

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:35.840
for the last year just because AI is just, it's, it's just moving the playing field

00:11:35.840 --> 00:11:39.359
so much and so often and so quickly.

00:11:39.359 --> 00:11:43.559
And uh, and I, it's, I don't think it's, it's just me, it's just us at Posit.

00:11:43.559 --> 00:11:45.119
It's the whole industry.

00:11:45.119 --> 00:11:51.239
I think, um, it, it's yeah, it's just like a time of fear, like excitement, yes, but

00:11:51.239 --> 00:11:57.479
also like this just existential dread and worry that underlies everything.

00:11:57.479 --> 00:12:03.239
I find that framing so interesting and there's so much to unpack there, but I, it does also

00:12:03.239 --> 00:12:09.799
kind of remind me of your like 2022 RStudio Conf keynote, where you talk a little bit

00:12:09.799 --> 00:12:15.640
about, um, creating Shiny and some of that environment.

00:12:15.640 --> 00:12:22.239
Like as I understand it, you mentioned like being on the verge of quitting essentially

00:12:22.239 --> 00:12:27.719
before kind of like hitting that, um, key idea.

00:12:27.719 --> 00:12:32.719
So I almost wonder, like, I'm really interested to unpack a lot of what you said about fear

00:12:32.719 --> 00:12:37.719
in the context of AI and in today, but, but to get there, I'm almost, I'm curious if you

00:12:37.719 --> 00:12:43.280
could bring us back to like 2012 and some of what you mentioned at the keynote, like

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:51.679
that, like mix of curiosity, duty, and fear in 2020, 2012 was Shiny.

00:12:51.679 --> 00:12:52.679
It's funny.

00:12:52.679 --> 00:12:54.039
I didn't make a connection to that moment, but you're exactly right.

00:12:54.039 --> 00:12:58.880
Like that's exactly a perfect illustration of what I'm talking about, where I think,

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:04.080
um, I started working at RStudio with JJ in 20, uh, 2009.

00:13:04.080 --> 00:13:09.960
So by 2012, it had been about three years and, um, at the time three years felt like

00:13:09.960 --> 00:13:10.960
a long time to me.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:16.919
Uh, but also I just, I felt like I've been working with JJ for a number of years.

00:13:16.919 --> 00:13:22.679
I think the world of JJ, I really, I mean, it was, the whole point was to work with him.

00:13:22.679 --> 00:13:27.760
That was the whole reason for joining RStudio was to keep working with JJ, but I had this

00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:33.320
feeling like, is this all that I get to be?

00:13:33.320 --> 00:13:40.359
You know, um, a lot of my friends were, uh, at that point starting companies or exiting

00:13:40.359 --> 00:13:43.440
companies, you know, um, like getting successful exits.

00:13:43.440 --> 00:13:50.919
Others were, um, I had a friend who was like the head of project, uh, or product management

00:13:50.919 --> 00:13:51.919
for Android.

00:13:51.919 --> 00:13:52.919
You know what I mean?

00:13:52.919 --> 00:14:01.099
Like just people who were like my classmates and peers that I was like, if I don't start

00:14:01.099 --> 00:14:03.020
a company, who am I?

00:14:03.020 --> 00:14:07.020
Like, like, can I even say that I've been successful?

00:14:07.020 --> 00:14:10.659
And I was really consumed by that.

00:14:10.659 --> 00:14:16.900
Like I think what I would now call FOMO, you know, um, but at the time I just felt like

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:18.539
I'm just, am I okay with this?

00:14:18.539 --> 00:14:24.260
Like I'm just going to be a failure, not a failure, but like my, my world is so small

00:14:24.260 --> 00:14:28.700
compared to, um, you know, people I considered peers.

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:36.619
So I didn't have an idea, uh, like I didn't know what kind of company I wanted to start.

00:14:36.619 --> 00:14:44.099
I just knew like, I need to be a founder otherwise, um, I won't consider myself successful.

00:14:44.099 --> 00:14:50.140
And um, yeah, like JJ was super supportive about it and he was like, okay, I mean, like

00:14:50.140 --> 00:14:55.219
I think you should have an idea first, but like whatever you decide, I'll, I'll support

00:14:55.219 --> 00:14:56.619
you in that.

00:14:56.619 --> 00:15:02.099
And um, yeah, it was, I, I, I committed to him, like I will be with you and focus only

00:15:02.099 --> 00:15:06.299
on our studio until the last day of the USAR conference that year.

00:15:06.299 --> 00:15:11.179
And I woke up on the last day, um, you know, basically the last day before I was going

00:15:11.179 --> 00:15:17.099
to start a several week soul searching process that was going to hopefully turn into, I have

00:15:17.099 --> 00:15:20.820
a killer idea and I leave and, you know, start that company.

00:15:20.820 --> 00:15:23.739
Um, and that was the day I thought of Shiny.

00:15:23.739 --> 00:15:27.619
And I think I described it as like a switch being flipped.

00:15:27.619 --> 00:15:36.820
Like it really was like a night and day difference before and after that, um, that like thunderbolt

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:48.979
of, of, um, Shiny struck me and like that I had thought that this was about me really

00:15:48.979 --> 00:15:55.380
wanting to start a company really, whatever, but it just, I don't know that, that a technical

00:15:55.380 --> 00:16:01.700
idea could so completely erase that feeling, um, of needing to prove myself, needing to

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:04.820
go do this, needing to measure up to my peers.

00:16:04.820 --> 00:16:08.260
Um, I never would have, would have predicted that.

00:16:08.260 --> 00:16:10.460
So that, I mean, it really was like a different mode.

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:16.179
Like my brain entered like, okay, let's build, you know, um, and who cares, who cares what

00:16:16.179 --> 00:16:17.780
your title is while you're building it?

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:23.179
Who cares, uh, you know, what the, what people are going to, whether people are going to

00:16:23.179 --> 00:16:26.179
credit you for this or whether you get to call yourself a founder.

00:16:26.179 --> 00:16:30.820
Um, it was just like, let's build, like, this is going to be awesome if it works.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:35.299
Like how could we not, how could we not just focus all of our energy on, on doing this?

00:16:35.299 --> 00:16:40.099
Um, that was like a huge lesson for me that like the thing that you think is the problem,

00:16:40.099 --> 00:16:43.859
the thing you, the thing that you think is causing you unrest, like maybe it's not the

00:16:43.859 --> 00:16:44.859
thing.

00:16:44.940 --> 00:16:51.299
But the biggest takeaway is like, I feel like it would have been miserable starting a company

00:16:51.299 --> 00:16:52.299
to start a company.

00:16:52.299 --> 00:16:55.500
It would have been miserable trying to be a successful founder so that I could measure

00:16:55.500 --> 00:16:59.780
up to, um, by the way, people that I will never catch.

00:16:59.780 --> 00:17:02.380
Like I could not have kept caught had I started my own thing.

00:17:02.380 --> 00:17:08.380
I truly believe that, uh, who are just born to do, you know, big things.

00:17:08.380 --> 00:17:14.140
And I think I was born to build little medium size, little to medium sized things.

00:17:14.140 --> 00:17:20.660
Um, and that in, when the right idea strikes, that's more than enough for me, you know.

00:17:20.660 --> 00:17:25.500
It's interesting to hear too, like, if I understand you're saying that at the time, like a lot

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:31.540
of your framing was like, I need to start a company and be successful or do a big thing.

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:36.420
But looking back, it sounds like you're saying like, you really appreciate the level of support

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:41.579
that you had at the time to kind of find something and to work on it that actually

00:17:41.579 --> 00:17:47.619
like you're realizing like the position you're in was pretty good and actually like a good

00:17:47.619 --> 00:17:48.619
fit for you.

00:17:48.619 --> 00:17:49.619
Yes.

00:17:49.619 --> 00:17:50.900
I think there are a couple of reasons.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:55.459
Like the first was, yes, I didn't, I didn't have to do all the other work that comes with

00:17:55.459 --> 00:17:56.859
starting a company.

00:17:56.859 --> 00:18:05.540
Um, the second is like having JJ there, I have like a built in co-founder and I know,

00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:07.859
I mean, Hadley knows this about me.

00:18:07.859 --> 00:18:13.380
Well, like I, I have very high levels of self-doubt, uh, which is often helpful, you know, like

00:18:13.380 --> 00:18:18.579
it's often helpful because it, it stops me from getting too lost in my own blind spots

00:18:18.579 --> 00:18:19.579
most of the time.

00:18:19.579 --> 00:18:25.859
But to be a true, especially solo founder, you need conviction, uh, and you need it all

00:18:25.859 --> 00:18:26.859
the time.

00:18:26.859 --> 00:18:28.680
Um, that's very difficult for me.

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:34.020
And then the third was like, I didn't want to build this and not have people use it.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:38.979
And we had our studio, like it was working, you know, we had a community that was building

00:18:38.979 --> 00:18:44.420
and I, it was more important to me that this thing launched to a receptive audience than

00:18:44.420 --> 00:18:49.819
it was for me to, I don't know, have a hundred percent of the equity or whatever it was,

00:18:49.819 --> 00:18:53.819
you know, um, that, that might've been competing with that priority.

00:18:53.819 --> 00:18:56.819
So yeah, I, I never, I didn't even really give it that much thought.

00:18:56.819 --> 00:18:59.900
It was like, as soon as it was, oh, this is the idea.

00:18:59.900 --> 00:19:02.819
It was like, yeah, I'm, I'm obviously doing it here.

00:19:02.819 --> 00:19:07.020
I mean, shiny is like vastly more successful than most startups.

00:19:07.020 --> 00:19:12.459
Like, even if you just like looked at the impact of like shiny, um, Bama, that's, that's

00:19:12.459 --> 00:19:15.579
like pretty, pretty incredible.

00:19:15.579 --> 00:19:20.979
No, it happened like pretty quickly, like it was like that, like people like that was

00:19:20.979 --> 00:19:27.140
like, when I remember when I first heard about Joe first heard about shiny, I was like, oh,

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:30.140
a web development framework for, ah, wow.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:32.739
That's something I really need.

00:19:32.739 --> 00:19:36.939
Uh, but then it like, it turns out actually that is what I need.

00:19:36.939 --> 00:19:42.420
Like people need a way to like in their ideas and the little interactive apps.

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:49.500
And it was like such a killer, like it just made like data scientists look so impressive

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:52.699
to people because they're like, oh, I just thought you were this like data scientist

00:19:52.699 --> 00:19:55.180
and now you're an app developer.

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:59.420
Like I remember like people in the early days who were like, you know, you shiny saved me

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:06.099
$200,000 because I prototype, I could prototype this app in a weekend instead of hiring a

00:20:06.099 --> 00:20:07.579
developer to do it for me.

00:20:07.579 --> 00:20:11.099
Like it was, yeah, pretty crazy.

00:20:11.099 --> 00:20:17.300
I guess jumping forward, it seems like 2022 was another really kind of big point where

00:20:17.300 --> 00:20:24.500
you were really focused on like shiny for Python and our, our studio, I think at that

00:20:24.500 --> 00:20:30.739
same cover, I think you announced in that keynote, uh, our studio changing its name

00:20:30.739 --> 00:20:31.739
deposit.

00:20:31.739 --> 00:20:33.780
Uh, Hadley and JJ announced it.

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:34.780
Yeah.

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:35.780
That morning.

00:20:35.780 --> 00:20:36.780
Yeah.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:37.780
That's right.

00:20:37.780 --> 00:20:38.780
Yeah.

00:20:38.780 --> 00:20:39.780
What was 2022 like then?

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:46.819
Like thinking about what, what do Python users need and kind of that, how was that for you?

00:20:46.819 --> 00:20:47.819
Yeah.

00:20:47.819 --> 00:20:52.739
Um, it was, yeah, a really fascinating couple of years there.

00:20:52.739 --> 00:20:55.619
It's a complicated story because we've done Python stuff for a while, right?

00:20:55.619 --> 00:20:59.540
Like we did, um, we did reticulate and there was some Python support in our studio, but

00:20:59.540 --> 00:21:04.020
I think it was, it was clearly like, this is an interop story, not a, we're not like

00:21:04.020 --> 00:21:12.459
actually targeting, um, you know, making Python users lives better, uh, until like 2021, 2022.

00:21:12.459 --> 00:21:17.500
And, um, we had talked about it many times.

00:21:17.500 --> 00:21:21.339
I think like at a lot of company work weeks, you know, the leadership team would get together

00:21:21.339 --> 00:21:26.540
and look at each other and be like, Python, Python, anyone, you know, and, um, and it

00:21:26.540 --> 00:21:32.260
always felt for years, we kept saying, no, we can't afford to dilute our focus.

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:35.979
There's so much to do for R that we can't afford to dilute our focus.

00:21:35.979 --> 00:21:40.060
Um, and also like, what kind of message is this going to send to the community?

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.859
Like is this, does this really make sense for us to do?

00:21:42.859 --> 00:21:48.900
And, um, what I recall, you correct me if I'm wrong, Hadley, is that when we started

00:21:48.900 --> 00:21:53.500
talking about the idea of Posit being around for the very, very long term, right?

00:21:53.500 --> 00:21:55.780
This is not just a company we want to be around for 10 years.

00:21:55.780 --> 00:21:59.660
We want it to potentially be around for a hundred years.

00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:06.020
Uh, that was when it went from, should we do stuff with Python to when do we do stuff

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:07.020
with Python?

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:16.260
Because it just became clear if we truly believe that we want to be around for a hundred years,

00:22:16.260 --> 00:22:17.979
we can't be a one language company.

00:22:17.979 --> 00:22:20.260
That just like, it doesn't make sense.

00:22:20.260 --> 00:22:21.260
It won't even be Python.

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:23.380
It was not going to be the terminal node.

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:26.619
This is just, this is just a journey that we're on and we're going to get through many

00:22:26.619 --> 00:22:27.619
languages.

00:22:27.619 --> 00:22:32.699
So, you know, may as well, it's just a question of when do we start, you know, n equals one

00:22:32.699 --> 00:22:34.420
becoming n equals two.

00:22:34.420 --> 00:22:38.859
Um, so I think in that sense, it went from a very difficult decision to a very, very

00:22:38.859 --> 00:22:40.579
easy decision.

00:22:40.579 --> 00:22:44.020
And then it was just a question of how do we do this?

00:22:44.020 --> 00:22:46.859
Like how do we kind of reach Python users where they are?

00:22:46.859 --> 00:22:48.579
And to be honest, like it is a challenge for us.

00:22:48.579 --> 00:22:53.219
It continues to be a challenge in a way that is very different than the challenge with

00:22:53.219 --> 00:23:01.339
R. I think R was a difficult, um, it was difficult because R was not seen as a language that

00:23:01.339 --> 00:23:04.300
was for a bunch of the things that we thought it should be for.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:08.859
Um, so maybe we had, we had to overcome skepticism that R was even for this.

00:23:08.859 --> 00:23:12.180
And we had to demonstrate like, no, R is amazing for this.

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:17.660
And over time when those battles, um, you know, one heart and mind at a time, Python

00:23:17.660 --> 00:23:22.579
is almost the exact opposite problem where like Python is acknowledged to be good at

00:23:22.579 --> 00:23:25.099
so many different things.

00:23:25.099 --> 00:23:30.819
And in any corner of the Python universe, there's six things vying to be the solution

00:23:30.819 --> 00:23:32.859
for that thing.

00:23:32.859 --> 00:23:39.060
Every once in a while, one thing just completely dominates like UV recently.

00:23:39.060 --> 00:23:44.099
But I think it's like much more normal for there just to be, you know, a lot of, a lot

00:23:44.099 --> 00:23:50.020
of different options and, and, um, like Python users just have to make sense out of many,

00:23:50.020 --> 00:23:54.420
many different, um, you know, competing ideas and competing viewpoints.

00:23:54.420 --> 00:24:02.140
So I think for us, the challenge was and is how do we take, um, we don't want to be too

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:06.099
literal in how we transfer the lessons from R over to Python.

00:24:06.099 --> 00:24:07.099
Python is a different language.

00:24:07.099 --> 00:24:08.699
It has different idioms and different norms.

00:24:09.339 --> 00:24:14.619
So we don't, we don't want to slavishly copy, um, you know, what, what we have been doing

00:24:14.619 --> 00:24:19.939
in our world, but at the same time, we do have a point of view, right?

00:24:19.939 --> 00:24:29.180
Um, like we may not, um, we, we may not literally want Shiny to work exactly the same way that

00:24:29.180 --> 00:24:34.939
it does in R, but we believe that this reactive model is useful for a large class of applications

00:24:34.939 --> 00:24:37.020
that are relevant to data scientists.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:42.099
You know, um, like we feel that there's something really fundamentally good about the way literate

00:24:42.099 --> 00:24:47.020
programming worked with RMDs and we want to, um, you know, make that available to QMDs,

00:24:47.020 --> 00:24:51.680
but with a totally different architecture, um, and yeah, just like working in a totally

00:24:51.680 --> 00:24:56.339
different way with the Jupyter kernels versus with, you know, in our execution engine.

00:24:56.339 --> 00:25:02.300
So, um, you can stop me at any time, cause I could go on about this for a while.

00:25:02.819 --> 00:25:08.619
But I think one thing that I do want to point out that I feel like, um, it is, uh, I think

00:25:08.619 --> 00:25:15.619
Jonathan McPherson said during, um, last year's PositConf keynote that he gave that the tools

00:25:15.619 --> 00:25:20.260
we build shape our thinking or the tools that we use shape our thinking.

00:25:20.260 --> 00:25:27.180
And there is, it has been interesting to realize once you hold these two ecosystems up right

00:25:27.180 --> 00:25:33.140
next to each other, how much our users thinking is shaped by what the tools can do and how

00:25:33.140 --> 00:25:35.020
much that's true for Python as well.

00:25:35.020 --> 00:25:40.900
And also true of the limitations of these tools, um, like not to pick on any particular.

00:25:42.699 --> 00:25:50.380
There are certain Python packages say for, you know, building data oriented web apps

00:25:51.140 --> 00:25:57.979
that someone coming from Shiny for R would look at that and be like, that'll never work.

00:25:58.459 --> 00:26:02.020
How do you handle X, Y, and Z critical scenarios?

00:26:02.619 --> 00:26:09.699
So it, it, it is a really interesting challenge for us to reconcile these, these two value

00:26:09.699 --> 00:26:15.500
systems and, um, and kind of learn by doing and, and see what resonates with people and, and

00:26:15.500 --> 00:26:20.619
see what doesn't, but it, it, it has been surprising over and over again as, as we've

00:26:20.739 --> 00:26:22.099
engaged with the Python community.

00:26:23.420 --> 00:26:23.739
Yeah.

00:26:24.060 --> 00:26:24.180
Yeah.

00:26:24.180 --> 00:26:24.859
It's so interesting.

00:26:24.859 --> 00:26:31.420
I also love if you throw, um, like dbt's community, the like SQL dbt community in the

00:26:31.420 --> 00:26:37.300
mix, now you have like, uh, three, a three piece stew.

00:26:37.739 --> 00:26:38.140
Yeah.

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:40.380
And then let's layer AI on top of that too.

00:26:40.380 --> 00:26:43.020
I mean, yeah, exactly.

00:26:43.260 --> 00:26:47.180
And it does feel like SQL is going to be our N equals three, I think.

00:26:47.180 --> 00:26:47.819
A hundred percent.

00:26:48.380 --> 00:26:49.819
Yeah, but not yet.

00:26:49.819 --> 00:26:54.219
But like that is a direction we're moving across so many different projects.

00:26:54.219 --> 00:26:58.339
Like if like every, every dataset just has to use SQL.

