00:29:46.859 --> 00:29:48.979
holding the wrong opinion for too long.

00:29:48.979 --> 00:29:50.619
Cause I'm not listening to people.

00:29:51.219 --> 00:29:57.180
But also, I mean, I, I was going to caveat that by saying you don't hold onto ideas

00:29:57.180 --> 00:29:57.780
too hard.

00:29:57.819 --> 00:30:01.500
Like if someone does prevent you present you with what you consider real evidence that

00:30:01.500 --> 00:30:04.459
you might be wrong, like you're very quick to change your mind.

00:30:04.739 --> 00:30:09.060
It's just that like in the absence of that evidence, you're not going to be shy about

00:30:09.060 --> 00:30:14.660
like, well, I understand that you feel that way, but I'm going to go with this cause

00:30:14.660 --> 00:30:15.819
I believe that it's right.

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:24.859
And, um, and I think my sort of politeness drive and like, let's all get a long drive.

00:30:25.540 --> 00:30:31.060
Um, makes it much historically it's been much more difficult for me to be bluntly

00:30:31.099 --> 00:30:32.780
like, okay, agree to disagree.

00:30:32.780 --> 00:30:33.699
We're going to do it my way.

00:30:34.099 --> 00:30:40.780
Uh, so like a lot of shiny tickets would kind of Peter out, you know, like just maybe

00:30:40.780 --> 00:30:42.300
if I ignore this, it'll go away.

00:30:42.490 --> 00:30:48.810
just maybe if I ignore this, it'll go away. And I like to think that I really have learned

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:51.610
from you, Hadley, in this, in the value of...

00:30:49.461 --> 00:30:55.941
just maybe if I ignore this it'll go away. And I like to think that I really have learned from

00:30:55.941 --> 00:31:01.621
you Hadley in this, in the value of just say it, you know, just say it. It's going to be fine.

00:31:01.621 --> 00:31:05.941
People will actually appreciate it. Like people, most people do not get mad. Some people get very

00:31:05.941 --> 00:31:12.420
mad, but most people don't get mad about it. And oh my gosh, like double and triple that

00:31:12.981 --> 00:31:19.141
if these are people who work for you. I mean, like it is a moral obligation to be clear.

00:31:19.941 --> 00:31:24.341
And not the light. That has been a painful lesson for me to learn over the years.

00:31:25.541 --> 00:31:30.420
But it's just so interesting. I think that like it feels like you were born this way,

00:31:30.420 --> 00:31:35.220
Hadley, you know. And I was born like in a very different attitude about this.

00:31:37.220 --> 00:31:41.861
Which I don't think I actually was. Yeah, like I don't know how I got to this

00:31:42.821 --> 00:31:48.900
point of certitude, but. When do you think you hit that point, Hadley? How did you,

00:31:48.900 --> 00:31:54.660
when did you crest Certitude Mountain, you know? I don't know. I kind of feel like a lot of that

00:31:54.660 --> 00:32:01.380
like I got when I got to RStudios. Maybe it was like suddenly like, oh, these people I respect

00:32:01.380 --> 00:32:07.700
now like trust my opinions. Like maybe I do know what I'm talking about. I just assumed that,

00:32:07.781 --> 00:32:14.660
you know, there was some old crusty professor who was berating you. And in that moment you were like,

00:32:16.501 --> 00:32:22.420
never again. We'll never bend the knee to a lesser idea or whatever.

00:32:23.300 --> 00:32:30.901
I have realized like I am primarily driven by like anti-role models. Like there are not many people

00:32:30.901 --> 00:32:35.380
I can point to where it'd be like, I want to be like them. There are lots of people I can point

00:32:35.380 --> 00:32:45.461
to. I'm like, I want to be the opposite of that. Interesting. I love it's like the machine learning

00:32:45.461 --> 00:32:51.781
model where it's like classifying binary classifier, but it's in reverse. So you just flip it.

00:32:53.541 --> 00:33:02.740
You're like, not that, not that, not that. Yeah, that's wild. I think this also gets to Joe.

