00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:12.181
I was able to, like in my first full-time job within a few months, I was managing a team of,

00:34:12.740 --> 00:34:19.380
I had two reports and it didn't take me long to be like, Oh no, like this, this is not for me.

00:34:19.380 --> 00:34:25.061
Like, I'm not the right shape to do this. This feels hard and bad. And,

00:34:26.821 --> 00:34:31.940
and yeah, I had to let someone kind of counsel someone out within the first few months. I was

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:37.300
like a 22 year old. I mean, I just, everything about that job, I was like, I would so much

00:34:37.300 --> 00:34:43.461
rather be coding and it would be better for the company if I was coding. So ever since then I've

00:34:43.461 --> 00:34:53.220
really resisted being a lead or a manager of any kind, but circumstances keep compelling me

00:34:53.781 --> 00:34:59.300
to step into that role. Just because like, to be honest, every time it's sort of been like,

00:35:00.981 --> 00:35:06.181
well, it's not ideal, but Joe's going to have to do this. You know, like nobody looks at me

00:35:06.181 --> 00:35:14.181
and thinks like, Oh, let's bring in our top notch management talent, Joe, you know? It's always more

00:35:14.181 --> 00:35:19.381
like, well, it seems like it can't be anyone else. So it's, we're going to have to settle

00:35:19.381 --> 00:35:27.221
for like having Joe lead this. That was the case with Shiny. I think that is a very negative

00:35:27.221 --> 00:35:36.341
characterization. I can bring, I can bring a lot of evidence. I think that, you know,

00:35:37.621 --> 00:35:42.820
I can say that because I think we've tried, we've tried many times, like, or at the very least,

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:49.621
at the very least people are like, let's, let's give Joe a break and have someone else lead. But

00:35:49.621 --> 00:35:53.941
there's always, there always seem to be reasons why that doesn't work out. I think in the early

00:35:53.941 --> 00:35:59.540
days of Shiny, there was just, I had such a clear vision in my head and it probably took like four

00:35:59.540 --> 00:36:06.501
or five years before just the set of things that I like in my head were already built, but they

00:36:06.501 --> 00:36:12.260
needed to be like actually built. Like that would have been a hard environment for someone else to

00:36:12.260 --> 00:36:19.221
step in to a place of leadership when like, I created this project, like I needed to review

00:36:19.221 --> 00:36:25.140
all the PRs. I hired all the people, like it was like the more I played that role, the more it

00:36:25.140 --> 00:36:30.661
ingrained me into that role and made it hard for someone else to come in and, and take my place.

00:36:32.980 --> 00:36:38.181
And I think the same thing happened with the AI stuff that I'm working on now. I'm leading a team

00:36:39.140 --> 00:36:45.461
doing sort of focusing on AI within Posit. I mean, every, every team is working on AI in some

00:36:45.461 --> 00:36:52.420
way, but there is a team that is specifically like, this is what we eat and breathe in and dream

00:36:52.420 --> 00:37:00.980
and sleep about AI. And yeah, I think once again, it was like, who else could run this, you know,

00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:08.740
given the shape of, of the charter of this team and what needs to be done. So I'm always like

00:37:08.740 --> 00:37:14.341
looking for ways to, especially the CTO title, I'd really prefer not to be CTO. I've made that

00:37:14.341 --> 00:37:19.621
pretty clear to leadership and it's just like, this is kind of how it has to be for the foreseeable

00:37:19.621 --> 00:37:30.020
future. So like in my, in my fantasies, I imagine, you know, someday having my, and my title go back

00:37:30.020 --> 00:37:35.941
to engineer and having like someone that I really, you know, trust and enjoy working with be the

00:37:35.941 --> 00:37:42.181
manager that I report to, and just, you know, being able to focus on the part of the job that,

00:37:42.181 --> 00:37:48.501
that I, I most enjoy. But I feel like we're not close to that being the right thing for the

00:37:48.501 --> 00:37:54.820
company, you know? So until that, until that happens, or I just hit my limit and I'm like,

00:37:54.820 --> 00:38:02.181
I can't do this anymore. It kind of has to be what it is. But I will say I have, once again,

00:38:02.181 --> 00:38:07.540
I think like Hadley and I have talked about this a lot. And one thing that has made it much easier

00:38:07.540 --> 00:38:15.540
is like learning from Hadley, like Hadley manages teams in a way that works for him.

00:38:16.101 --> 00:38:20.820
I was going to say, if you wanted to characterize my leadership style, it would be benign neglect.

00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:25.061
You're giving people autonomy. You're letting them thrive. Is that?

00:38:26.661 --> 00:38:32.341
Like, I don't know. I think like Joe, Joe is a better leader than me, but he also cares

00:38:32.341 --> 00:38:39.381
about it more than I do. So I'm just like, I'm not, I'm like, whatever, someone's going to do it.

00:38:40.101 --> 00:38:43.061
Yeah. Better than malignant neglect, I guess.

00:38:43.621 --> 00:38:45.941
Yeah, exactly. Better than malign neglect.

00:38:46.980 --> 00:38:56.260
But I think Hadley, what makes it work is that the people that work for you, that is actually what

00:38:56.260 --> 00:39:06.181
they prefer and enjoy. And it's shown in the tenure that we have on your team, on a lot of our

00:39:06.181 --> 00:39:11.461
teams, like it's working, you know, like this is the way that they want to be managed. And it would

00:39:11.461 --> 00:39:17.221
be really, it would feel really bad to be managed by a more traditional, you know, scrum, you know,

00:39:17.221 --> 00:39:25.781
agile, whatever. And I think being willing to be clear eyed about what do I need as the manager,

00:39:25.781 --> 00:39:32.180
like, what do I need this team to be in order for this to work for me? And how can I select people

00:39:32.180 --> 00:39:38.020
for this team that that's going to work for them as well, instead of I think what I thought before,

00:39:38.020 --> 00:39:44.741
which is, if I can imagine this person being a big contributor on my team, then I will become

00:39:44.741 --> 00:39:50.820
whatever shape I need to, like, I will be the universal adapter for all of the spiky shaped

00:39:50.820 --> 00:39:57.541
individuals on my team. And it is my job to fill in all the gaps to do whatever emotional work with

00:39:57.541 --> 00:40:03.060
them needs to be done for them to be effective, to fill in whatever technical gaps they might be

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:11.461
deficient at for them to be effective. And I, you know, I did that for like a number of years. And

00:40:12.421 --> 00:40:21.140
that was, you know, a recipe for burnout kind of predictably. And also, I think didn't work great

00:40:21.140 --> 00:40:30.901
for the people that I was doing this feeling like I was doing this in service of, you know, so this

00:40:30.901 --> 00:40:35.461
latest iteration of both the Shiny team and the AI core team.

00:40:35.461 --> 00:40:40.421
I'm really surprised. I thought you were saying that that worked for you. So I at least just want

00:40:40.580 --> 00:40:47.700
to dwell there for a bit, because I, I thought you were saying, like, when I gap fill for a person

00:40:47.700 --> 00:40:54.341
who's like really effective, I feel like I'm really doing a good job. But it sounds like that

00:40:54.341 --> 00:40:59.461
you mentioned, like, burned you out a bit and kind of didn't work for them. Like what?

00:41:01.781 --> 00:41:02.661
What went wrong?

00:41:03.380 --> 00:41:12.180
I think it, it is rarely as cut and dry as like, this person just needs this, you know,

00:41:12.180 --> 00:41:17.301
they're great at everything except for X. If I can provide X, they're like incredibly effective.

00:41:17.301 --> 00:41:22.741
What it looks like more of the time is I have this person that is a middling amount of effective.

00:41:23.861 --> 00:41:31.861
How many different things can I try to, you know, to compensate for that or to,

00:41:31.861 --> 00:41:36.421
to align them in a way that they can be maximally effective. And it is not

00:41:38.500 --> 00:41:43.700
a feedback loop with super tight intervals. It is not a feedback loop with super clear signals.

00:41:44.260 --> 00:41:51.140
And instead, like, it feels, I mean, it feels a lot like parenting, maybe. It feels like

00:41:52.260 --> 00:41:57.781
I'm doing a lot of work to really understand someone, like, and not just what they're saying

00:41:57.781 --> 00:42:02.341
is the problem, but what is the problem behind the problem? You know, sometimes like, it feels

00:42:02.341 --> 00:42:08.180
like almost like we have to do therapy to get to the bottom of like, what is standing in your way,

00:42:08.180 --> 00:42:16.901
or why are you feeling so angsty about this or that? And that is really exhausting. It's

00:42:16.901 --> 00:42:25.541
really exhausting work when you're doing it for five, six, seven people. And if you finish that

00:42:25.541 --> 00:42:31.781
work and you don't see a big change, or you finish that work, and people don't recognize you

00:42:31.781 --> 00:42:40.341
as doing that work. And in fact, like that work not being legible to the other managers in the

00:42:40.341 --> 00:42:47.461
organization who really kind of just see that you're doing things in a weird way. And yeah,

00:42:47.461 --> 00:42:52.260
I've definitely come in for some criticism from the rest of the organization. So yeah, I think

00:42:53.221 --> 00:43:00.260
at this point in my career, I'm much more excited about finding people with compatible shapes to me.

00:43:01.301 --> 00:43:07.060
And one thing that's helped is that I'm more clear on what a compatible shape looks like to me and

00:43:07.060 --> 00:43:12.580
the kinds of people that I think can be very effective under my leadership, or even as a

00:43:12.580 --> 00:43:18.901
collaborator, and the kind of people who like, it's going to be a struggle. And if the stakes

00:43:19.700 --> 00:43:27.781
are high enough, I'm happy to say, sorry, you're going to have to change seats or you're going to

00:43:27.781 --> 00:43:34.421
have to get up so someone else can sit there, because it's what I will need for this to be

00:43:34.421 --> 00:43:37.620
a sustainable kind of an operation. Boy, this got very frank.

00:43:41.221 --> 00:43:46.180
No, I honestly think it's so helpful. And even you framed it as burnout,

00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:52.741
but maybe in another way, it's also kind of like being kind to the person. Like if they won't have

00:43:52.741 --> 00:44:00.661
a great time and in that spot, like, am I kind of like artificially propping them up versus giving

00:44:00.661 --> 00:44:08.260
them like frank feedback? So I often like think of gap filling is like really a really powerful

00:44:08.260 --> 00:44:15.461
strategy. So it's something that I'll definitely think about. Yeah, I mean, I think it can be

00:44:15.461 --> 00:44:21.620
really powerful, but it can also be like disenabling in the sense of like, if you were

00:44:21.620 --> 00:44:26.901
just going to like, if someone is not doing something that they like should learn how to do,

00:44:26.901 --> 00:44:32.820
and you just do it for them, like that, you know, that there's lots of reasons to do that in the

00:44:32.820 --> 00:44:37.700
short term, and it can be really powerful and useful, but in the long term, like that doesn't

00:44:38.421 --> 00:44:47.380
necessarily like serve you or serve them. Yeah, I think like, I don't know, like took me,

00:44:47.380 --> 00:44:51.940
I also had like, I don't know, it took me a long time to get to the point where I can like give

00:44:51.940 --> 00:44:58.500
people like direct feedback where they're like, you're like disappointing me, like you're not

00:44:58.500 --> 00:45:05.140
like this is not working the way it should do or it needs to work. That like that is like,

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:10.981
that is really tough to deliver, but it's also better than the alternative because typically

00:45:10.981 --> 00:45:15.700
people who are not doing well, like they know it, they can get that sense. You might not be

00:45:15.700 --> 00:45:20.661
saying it directly, but they know you're unhappy. And if they don't know like what is wrong,

00:45:20.661 --> 00:45:26.101
like they can't respond. Yeah. I'm curious your thoughts on today now where you mentioned you're

00:45:27.700 --> 00:45:32.260
leading an AI team. What, yeah. How do you think of things today?

00:45:33.140 --> 00:45:42.580
Yeah. Yeah. The first thing that struck me actually, it was, it was one of your

00:45:42.580 --> 00:45:48.020
questions you asked me earlier, Michael, before this recording, it really made me think about

00:45:48.020 --> 00:45:56.580
the fact that a lot of the earlier moments in my career, the exciting moments were just exciting.

00:45:56.580 --> 00:46:00.260
And this particular moment is exciting mixed with existential dread.

00:46:02.260 --> 00:46:11.301
And like that, that combination is so weird. Like it is such a strange sensation

00:46:12.341 --> 00:46:19.060
to build something that you're both incredibly excited about. And also should we even be,

00:46:19.060 --> 00:46:25.221
should we even be doing this? Like, is this the right thing long-term for the industry,

00:46:25.221 --> 00:46:35.541
for people who are going to use this for the planet? And yeah, I have felt more conflicted

00:46:35.541 --> 00:46:41.620
about this work over the last couple of years than like at any point in my career where the

00:46:41.620 --> 00:46:51.781
sort of push and pull of AI has like literally kept me awake at night wondering, you know,

00:46:51.781 --> 00:46:57.861
should I be doing this? Should I abstain from being involved in this technology at all?

00:47:00.020 --> 00:47:04.901
So that's the first thing I think that is really striking about this moment is how much

00:47:04.901 --> 00:47:09.301
sort of ethical soul-searching I feel like a lot of us have had to do.

00:47:11.060 --> 00:47:15.940
And for me, I've gotten to a place, we can talk about it if you want to, I've gotten to a place

00:47:15.940 --> 00:47:22.500
where I don't lose sleep about this. I'm quite convicted that the work that we are doing is not

00:47:22.500 --> 00:47:27.301
positive and it is important that we continue to do it. But the other, there are some other

00:47:27.301 --> 00:47:35.541
things that are really weird about this moment. This, like the technology, like LLMs as a sort

00:47:35.541 --> 00:47:42.741
of core technology, the fact that they're both so incredibly capable and until like very, very

00:47:42.741 --> 00:47:48.341
recently, I'd say so, so unintuitive, like it's so unintuitive what they're going to succeed at,

00:47:48.341 --> 00:47:57.141
what they're going to fail at, that it's almost impossible to come to any conclusion about whether

00:47:57.141 --> 00:48:01.300
something's going to be useful until after you've built it, after you've built it. Like it's hard

00:48:01.300 --> 00:48:05.540
to reason from first principles, whether you should harness them this way or that way.

00:48:06.660 --> 00:48:09.540
And then at the same time, because the tools that you build so fast,

00:48:10.501 --> 00:48:16.820
that's actually a viable strategy. Like we don't ponder on paper, we ponder

00:48:17.700 --> 00:48:27.381
with prototypes now, you know. And it's created this whole new motion of just, you know, my team

00:48:27.381 --> 00:48:34.820
and the teams that I'm in close contact with are just throwing off massive numbers of ideas

00:48:34.820 --> 00:48:41.700
every week. And it's no longer a generation problem, like generating features, generating

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:47.060
code. It's like a curation problem. It's a conviction problem, like developing conviction

00:48:47.060 --> 00:48:56.420
about what you think is right for the user. That is now the big struggle and ideally the key

00:48:56.420 --> 00:49:01.461
differentiator for us, because collectively we have so much experience in data science and we

00:49:01.461 --> 00:49:06.900
have like really strong opinions about the right and wrong ways to get answers. If correctness is

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:10.501
important to you and reproducibility is important to you, you know, we have a lot of opinions about

00:49:10.501 --> 00:49:16.261
that. And I think a lot of people, a lot of competitors or ostensibly competitors have

00:49:16.261 --> 00:49:23.221
different priorities, which gives us sort of a unique viewpoint to build around. But it's still,

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:31.540
like, it doesn't feel like any other motion in my career, you know, where it's the conviction

00:49:31.540 --> 00:49:37.221
and it's the editing. It's not like it takes you X amount of time to come up with the idea and then

00:49:37.221 --> 00:49:44.101
10X that amount of time to execute. It's really trying to think of like, what do we actually

00:49:44.101 --> 00:49:47.221
think is the right way to harness these things? And then the building of it is relatively

00:49:47.221 --> 00:49:51.861
straightforward. And then on top of that, it's changing all the time, right? Like every model,

00:49:52.420 --> 00:49:55.461
the lessons we're learning about building harnesses collectively as an industry, it's

00:49:55.461 --> 00:50:00.420
changing so fast that it adds another level of complication to try and to form conviction

00:50:00.420 --> 00:50:04.501
around things. Because you have to have conviction that it's going to stay constant for six months

00:50:05.381 --> 00:50:09.141
or hopefully more than that. So a really weird time.

00:50:09.780 --> 00:50:15.940
Is the thing that was keeping you up at night asking like, what would be useful for people?

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:16.820
Or was it something?

00:50:16.820 --> 00:50:20.180
No, it was, is it ethical to work on these things?

00:50:20.180 --> 00:50:20.420
Societally.

00:50:20.420 --> 00:50:21.221
Is it ethical?

00:50:21.221 --> 00:50:21.461
Yeah.

00:50:22.021 --> 00:50:29.060
Is it like considering the negative side effects of this technology existing,

00:50:29.060 --> 00:50:34.101
not to mention the negative potential side effects that especially two years ago were quite

00:50:34.981 --> 00:50:39.861
extreme, like people were, well, I guess some people still do, but like, it was much more in

00:50:39.861 --> 00:50:46.180
the discourse that these things could kill us all or some approximation of dooming the human race.

00:50:46.660 --> 00:50:52.900
And it certainly like, what effect is this going to have on knowledge work?

00:50:52.900 --> 00:50:57.780
What, what effect is like this amount of change this fast going to have on our economy and society

00:50:57.780 --> 00:51:08.420
and the copyright, you know, the copyright infringement, I guess that these models were

00:51:08.420 --> 00:51:11.221
mostly built on being an ethical problem.

00:51:12.021 --> 00:51:17.221
And then also, I think on top of that, in the particular domain of data science,

00:51:18.660 --> 00:51:22.261
the ability of these things to so quickly and easily give you

00:51:22.261 --> 00:51:24.261
answers that you don't know if they're correct.

00:51:25.540 --> 00:51:30.900
And feeling like, I don't know, we could build tools that are like handing out loaded guns

00:51:32.820 --> 00:51:34.101
to the general populace.

00:51:34.101 --> 00:51:37.060
And then just like having a warning sticker on it, like warning, this is a loaded gun.

00:51:37.060 --> 00:51:37.940
So we're cool, right?

00:51:37.940 --> 00:51:40.101
Like we did our job, we warned people.

00:51:40.101 --> 00:51:41.141
So it's really on them.

00:51:41.141 --> 00:51:45.060
But like, if you know that people are going to, you know, shoot their feet off or whatever

00:51:45.060 --> 00:51:49.700
with these guns, is it really ethical for you to release that like into the marketplace?

00:51:49.700 --> 00:51:57.621
So those were the kinds of questions I think that like, I really legitimately struggled with.

00:51:58.741 --> 00:52:03.861
Should I go to, you know, to Reef, our CEO and tell him like, I want to keep working here,

00:52:03.861 --> 00:52:05.540
but I don't want to work on AI anymore.

00:52:05.700 --> 00:52:12.341
I'm conscientious objector, or maybe even like, if this company is going to continue to

00:52:13.461 --> 00:52:19.381
develop AI, then, or develop solutions around AI, then I need to work somewhere else.

00:52:19.381 --> 00:52:23.060
Which is ironic, because I was literally the one leading the charge at the time, you know.

00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:27.621
So it was not a statement about whether I should continue to be neutral.

00:52:27.621 --> 00:52:32.741
It's like, I'm literally evangelizing this technology within the company and to our

00:52:32.741 --> 00:52:40.261
customers while feeling intensely conflicted about whether it was ethical to even

00:52:41.461 --> 00:52:44.261
have an open AI account or whatever.

00:52:46.101 --> 00:52:49.940
And I don't mean to say like, I knew that it was unethical.

00:52:50.741 --> 00:52:56.501
It was that I was really like riding that line of, I can't decide if this is like net

00:52:56.501 --> 00:53:01.700
very positive, net very negative, like catch me on a different day and I'll have a different answer.

00:53:02.180 --> 00:53:07.540
I think it's like so hard to kind of balance because it feels like the good things are

00:53:07.540 --> 00:53:09.861
like so good and the bad things are so bad.

00:53:09.861 --> 00:53:11.940
They're both like really large numbers.

00:53:11.940 --> 00:53:14.261
And what do they cancel out to?

00:53:14.261 --> 00:53:19.461
And like, I think our, at least your mindset now is like, they need out to something that's

00:53:19.461 --> 00:53:21.861
like, you know, mild to moderately positive.

00:53:22.741 --> 00:53:29.060
Like that doesn't mean there's still not like lots of really bad things, but maybe mostly

00:53:29.060 --> 00:53:31.700
kind of balanced out by the good things.

00:53:31.700 --> 00:53:38.580
And like we have the opportunity to kind of at least try and accentuate the positives

00:53:38.580 --> 00:53:43.621
and make the negatives worse for like some, make the negatives less worse for some group

00:53:43.621 --> 00:53:44.420
of people.

00:53:44.420 --> 00:53:45.060
That's right.

00:53:45.060 --> 00:53:45.940
That's right.

00:53:45.940 --> 00:53:46.741
Yeah.

00:53:46.741 --> 00:53:51.700
And I think for me, it's not so much like today's trade-offs, like the harms and benefits

00:53:51.700 --> 00:53:56.820
that have been realized today, I do agree are mild to moderately positive.

00:53:57.780 --> 00:54:04.501
It's the potential harms and the potential positives that really, like, it's like the

00:54:04.501 --> 00:54:09.540
error bars are like this, or the error bars are very wide, I guess, going forward.

00:54:10.820 --> 00:54:11.221
Yeah.

00:54:11.221 --> 00:54:11.780
Yeah.

00:54:11.780 --> 00:54:16.900
I think the other thing that makes it so hard to think about is it does feel like if we

00:54:16.900 --> 00:54:23.861
as a company don't buy into it, then like the company is not going to exist.

00:54:24.820 --> 00:54:31.861
And like how that, like, I think that just makes that it's so hard to tease apart that

00:54:31.861 --> 00:54:36.501
kind of like pure ethics from like, okay, well, I, you know, I like my job.

00:54:36.501 --> 00:54:38.741
I'd like to continue having this job.

00:54:38.741 --> 00:54:40.101
I like the people I work with.

00:54:40.101 --> 00:54:42.261
I like, they want them to keep having more jobs.

00:54:42.261 --> 00:54:48.021
And like, at what point are you willing to pull the fire alarm and say, like, like we

00:54:48.021 --> 00:54:53.780
have to stop this train, even an extent to that, like by not doing anything with AI,

00:54:53.780 --> 00:54:55.940
like, like that's the end of the business.

00:54:55.940 --> 00:55:01.940
And, you know, five Latinos like that just add all of this, like additional, like emotional

00:55:01.940 --> 00:55:02.741
weight to it.

00:55:02.741 --> 00:55:03.060
